# FORGENIQ autonomous execution

## Decide, don't ask
- Do not ask the user to choose between routine implementation or testing options; take the recommended option when it fits the approved architecture, security requirements and project objectives.
- Do not request approval again for activities already authorised.
- Record each decision, its reason and its outcome in `docs/uat/EXECUTION-LOG.md`.
- Continue through successive implementation and test stages; report progress in consolidated summaries, not a question after every step.

## Authorised without asking
- Local development, code fixes and routine bug fixes.
- Unit, integration and regression tests; root cause investigation; security and regulatory validation.
- Read-only staging database queries.
- Previously approved staging activities and continuing approved test sequences.
- Automated UAT as Users A, B and C through the saved sessions (`apps/platform/scripts/uat/staging-user-sessions.mjs`).
- Documentation, backlog, screenshot and evidence updates.

## Stop and ask only when
- Deploying to production.
- Applying a staging migration or deployment not already authorised.
- Performing a destructive database operation.
- Changing user permissions or organisation memberships.
- Incurring AI or infrastructure cost not already approved (e.g. a second paid extraction or any retry).
- Changing security architecture or a regulatory classification policy.
- A regulatory interpretation cannot be resolved from authoritative sources.
- Authentication needs the user (e.g. a saved session has expired).
- A critical test fails and continuing could compromise data integrity.

## Never
- Bypass security controls, segregation of duties or approval requirements.
- Expose credentials, tokens or session state, or commit authentication state.
- Fabricate obligations or regulatory requirements.
